## Projects

### 📊 Testing the "Lipstick Effect" — Economic Cycles and Cinema Demand

Does cinema demand rise in downturns, as the "lipstick effect" suggests? I
combined 70 years of Japanese box-office statistics (Motion Picture Producers
Association of Japan, 1955–2025) with economic indicators — real GDP and the
Consumer Confidence Index.

**Findings:** The hypothesis was not supported. A positive correlation seen in
raw values disappeared once tested on year-over-year changes, revealing it as a
spurious correlation driven by a shared time trend. Cinema demand turned out to
be shaped far more by structural factors (the spread of television and streaming)
than by economic cycles.

`Python` · `pandas` · `statsmodels` · `matplotlib`
Methods: multi-source data integration · OLS regression · growth-rate & lagged
analysis · spurious-correlation testing · robustness check (outlier removal)

